window.docContents[177].resolve({"/Constructions/#chapter-constructions":{"contents":"namespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nIn this chapter we will introduce some of the concrete examples of simple graphs that\ncan be found in Mathlib together with a few new ones. The idea is to give a flavour\nof the kinds of constructions that are possible in Mathlib. It also offers a good\nexcuse to introduce many of the fundamental types that may be unfamiliar to graph\ntheorists but are used throughout Mathlib. These include Bool, Fin, Option,\nProd, Sigma, Subtype, Sum and Unit.\n\nTwo of the constructions we introduce below, the\nhypercube and\nMycielskian, will serve as running examples throughout\nthe remaining chapters.\n\nBeware that in this chapter we are looking at constructions, and so we will see\nexamples of complete bipartite graphs and cycle graphs etc., but we won't see any\ndiscussion yet of what it means for a graph to be bipartite or to contain a cycle\netc.\n\nTo sanity-check our definitions, we will want to perform computations on small\nexamples using #eval and decide. We will look at such computations in much more\ndetail in the next chapter, but for them to work we need to provide decidability\ninstances for adjacency in the graphs we define. Feel free to ignore these and\nrevisit them once you have read the next chapter!\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"3. Constructions","id":"/Constructions/#chapter-constructions"},"/Subgraphs/#section-partial-colourings":{"contents":"Informally, a partial γ-colouring of G : SimpleGraph V should be a map\nC : V → γ that is valid on a given subset of the vertices s : Set V, where valid\nmeans something like ∀ a b ∈ s, G.Adj a b → C a ≠ C b.\n\nThere is currently no definition of partial colourings in Mathlib. However they arise\nin many situations, such as the proof of Brooks' theorem. (Indeed we will use them\nfor exactly this; see Brooks' Theorem.)\n\nBelow we discuss a possible implementation making use of the existing\nSimpleGraph.Coloring API where possible.\n\nWe want a partial colouring C to be a total function on V and we also want to\nreuse the SimpleGraph.Coloring API. The natural way to implement this is as a\ncolouring of the subgraph of G induced by s but with all vertices of V\nretained: i.e. a colouring of (G.induce s).spanningCoe, where spanningCoe is\nSimpleGraph.spanningCoe from SpanningCoe. Alternatively we could\nask for C to be a colouring of ((⊤ : G.Subgraph).induce s).spanningCoe, using\nSubgraph.spanningCoe instead.\n\nThese are the same graph (this is Mathlib's spanningCoe_induce_top), but they are\nnot definitionally equal\n(Definitional and Propositional Equalities), and the latter is\nmuch easier to work with. Its adjacency relation is definitionally\na ∈ s ∧ b ∈ s ∧ G.Adj a b. While in (G.induce s).spanningCoe, adjacency unfolds\nto an existential over vertices of the subtype ↑s, coming from SimpleGraph.map:\na ≠ b ∧ ∃ u v : ↑s, (G.induce s).Adj u v ∧ ↑u = a ∧ ↑v = b.\n\nsection partcol\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\n\nexample {s : Set V} :\n    ((⊤ : G.Subgraph).induce s).spanningCoe = (G.induce s).spanningCoe :=\n  spanningCoe_induce_top _\n\n\nexample {a b : V} {s : Set V} :\n    ((⊤ : G.Subgraph).induce s).spanningCoe.Adj a b ↔ a ∈ s ∧ b ∈ s ∧ G.Adj a b :=\n  Iff.rfl\n\n\nexample {a b : V} {s : Set V} :\n    (G.induce s).spanningCoe.Adj a b ↔\n      a ≠ b ∧ ∃ u v : ↑s, (G.induce s).Adj u v ∧ ↑u = a ∧ ↑v = b := Iff.rfl\n\n\nvariable (G)\n\n\nA ColoringOn γ s of G : SimpleGraph V is a γ-colouring of all vertices of\nG that is a valid colouring on the set s./-- A ColoringOn γ s of G : SimpleGraph V is a γ-colouring of all vertices of\nG that is a valid colouring on the set s. -/\nabbrev ColoringOn (γ : Type*) (s : Set V) :=\n  ((⊤ : G.Subgraph).induce s).spanningCoe.Coloring γ\n\n\nG.ColorableOn n s iff there is a total function V → Fin n that is a valid\ncolouring of G on the set s./-- G.ColorableOn n s iff there is a total function V → Fin n that is a valid\ncolouring of G on the set s. -/\nabbrev ColorableOn (n : ℕ) (s : Set V) := Nonempty (G.ColoringOn (Fin n) s)\n\n\nvariable {s t : Set V} {γ : Type*} {G}\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs","header":"9.6. Partial Colourings*","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-partial-colourings"}});